WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.520 --> 00:00:01.380
- [Ben] Hi, I'm Ben.

00:00:01.380 --> 00:00:03.000
- [Ahmed] Hi, I'm Ahmed and today

00:00:03.000 --> 00:00:04.220
we're making one of my favorite

00:00:04.220 --> 00:00:06.113
childhood dishes, chicken shawarma.

00:00:07.020 --> 00:00:09.550
- [Ben] It's actually one
of my childhood dishes also.

00:00:09.550 --> 00:00:11.753
I remember going by the ...

00:00:11.890 --> 00:00:14.210
Some are like, actually
pizza places in New York

00:00:14.210 --> 00:00:15.720
would also have the shawarma.

00:00:15.720 --> 00:00:17.880
- [Ahmed] It's funny, I feel like shawarma

00:00:17.940 --> 00:00:20.183
is to Aleppo what pizza is to New York.

00:00:20.570 --> 00:00:22.480
I just vividly remember the memories

00:00:22.480 --> 00:00:23.940
of my mom sending me to the store.

00:00:23.940 --> 00:00:26.440
You have the chicken, and then
you have this garlic sauce,

00:00:26.440 --> 00:00:28.120
which we'll be making later today.

00:00:28.120 --> 00:00:29.253
And, that's the magic.

00:00:29.460 --> 00:00:30.943
It's amazing, that's so good.

00:00:33.269 --> 00:00:34.102
- It really is good.

00:00:34.102 --> 00:00:35.820
- We should just do that
for the rest of the video.

00:00:35.820 --> 00:00:37.560
- [Ben] We met last year.

00:00:37.560 --> 00:00:38.880
- [Ahmed] Yeah, almost exactly a year ago.

00:00:38.880 --> 00:00:41.270
- [Ben] Yeah, at World
Refugee Day at the U.N.

00:00:41.270 --> 00:00:43.420
Because I had been doing
some work with UNHCR,

00:00:43.420 --> 00:00:44.810
Union Refugee Agency.

00:00:44.810 --> 00:00:46.040
You were reading a poem there.

00:00:46.040 --> 00:00:47.790
- [Ahmed] Yes, I wrote a poem called,

00:00:47.790 --> 00:00:49.210
A Thank You Letter, from the bomb

00:00:49.210 --> 00:00:50.720
that visited my home 11 years ago.

00:00:50.720 --> 00:00:52.720
So, it's from the perspective of the bomb

00:00:52.720 --> 00:00:54.923
talking to me after all these years.

00:00:55.340 --> 00:00:58.830
I was eight, eight years old.

00:00:58.830 --> 00:01:00.510
This was in 2006.

00:01:00.510 --> 00:01:02.330
So, we had to flee Iraq in 2006.

00:01:02.330 --> 00:01:04.320
We literally just got on a bus.

00:01:04.320 --> 00:01:06.660
And, as a kid, you don't
really realize these things.

00:01:06.660 --> 00:01:09.210
You don't understand that you
might be leaving your home

00:01:09.210 --> 00:01:10.483
for the very last time.

00:01:10.750 --> 00:01:12.720
- [Ben] And the first place you
lived in the U.S. was where?

00:01:12.720 --> 00:01:14.520
- [Ahmed] Sioux Falls, South Dakota.

00:01:14.570 --> 00:01:17.153
- [Ben] Wow, how's the
shawarma in Sioux Falls?

00:01:17.170 --> 00:01:18.820
- [Ahmed] Ah, it's not very good.

00:01:19.770 --> 00:01:21.500
We moved around a few times in the U.S.

00:01:21.500 --> 00:01:23.450
and shawarma wasn't always right there.

00:01:23.500 --> 00:01:25.120
Living in the states, and having to create

00:01:25.120 --> 00:01:28.280
this dish at home, as opposed
to getting it down the street.

00:01:28.280 --> 00:01:30.840
I should ask my dad, why
didn't we make life choices

00:01:30.840 --> 00:01:33.193
based on where shawarma
is readily available?

00:01:34.310 --> 00:01:35.839
- Pretty good, yeah.

00:01:35.839 --> 00:01:37.100
- There's like that little aftertaste.

00:01:37.100 --> 00:01:40.423
- Yeah, it has a little,
a little zing at the end.

00:01:40.440 --> 00:01:41.490
It's kind of perfect.

00:01:41.550 --> 00:01:42.383
- [Ahmed] Right.

00:01:43.550 --> 00:01:45.520
- [Ben] And seriously, how did you guys

00:01:45.610 --> 00:01:46.823
end up in South Dakota?

00:01:47.070 --> 00:01:48.450
- [Ahmed] When you flee your home,

00:01:48.450 --> 00:01:50.630
and then you apply for refugee status,

00:01:50.630 --> 00:01:52.910
it's a very, very, very long process.

00:01:52.910 --> 00:01:55.100
We found out one summer
day we were in Jubla.

00:01:55.100 --> 00:01:56.770
I remember I was in my bedroom,

00:01:56.770 --> 00:01:59.030
my mom was in the living
room, all of a sudden

00:01:59.030 --> 00:02:00.373
I just hear screaming.

00:02:00.420 --> 00:02:01.253
I'm like, "Oh my God.

00:02:01.253 --> 00:02:04.457
"Did she find out that I
did that thing yesterday?"

00:02:04.670 --> 00:02:06.850
You know and then, I walk in and then

00:02:06.980 --> 00:02:09.257
she kind of just puts the
phone down and she's like,

00:02:09.257 --> 00:02:11.160
"Ahmed, we're going to America."

00:02:11.160 --> 00:02:12.247
When they told her on the phone they said,

00:02:12.247 --> 00:02:13.737
"You have four one way tickets to

00:02:13.737 --> 00:02:15.137
"Sioux Falls, South Dakota."

00:02:15.400 --> 00:02:18.150
- [Ben] Idea of what a
refugee is in people's minds,

00:02:18.150 --> 00:02:20.100
there's an image there that doesn't really

00:02:20.100 --> 00:02:21.400
correlate with reality.

00:02:21.400 --> 00:02:22.790
It's not something you choose.

00:02:22.790 --> 00:02:24.100
They're looking for an opportunity

00:02:24.100 --> 00:02:25.040
just to have a life.

00:02:25.040 --> 00:02:25.873
- [Ahmed] Exactly.

00:02:25.873 --> 00:02:27.010
And, when we first came
to the states, you know,

00:02:27.010 --> 00:02:28.220
my parents had to find jobs.

00:02:28.220 --> 00:02:30.533
They had to figure out
how to make ends meet.

00:02:31.211 --> 00:02:33.310
Americans have to do that, as well.

00:02:33.310 --> 00:02:35.990
We have to start thinking
of the common thread

00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:37.070
through these experiences.

00:02:37.070 --> 00:02:39.683
It doesn't have to be this
foreign, foreign thing.

00:02:41.160 --> 00:02:42.490
- Cheers, okay.
- Cheers.

00:02:45.408 --> 00:02:47.958
There's this like, amazing
familiarity that happens

00:02:48.240 --> 00:02:49.633
like, right mid-bite.

00:02:50.200 --> 00:02:51.620
You're transported somewhere else,

00:02:51.620 --> 00:02:53.740
and you're reminded of not
only just like the food,

00:02:53.740 --> 00:02:55.633
but the people that prepared it.

00:02:55.710 --> 00:02:57.450
- Yeah, and really I'm just like

00:02:57.450 --> 00:03:02.213
struck by the, how much I like to eat.

00:03:02.770 --> 00:03:04.483
Food, I love food.

00:03:05.030 --> 00:03:06.323
- [Narrator] Oh, yes!

